  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 826,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 769,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124929,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 402,
        "headingId": 769,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These tumours involve distal extremities and trunk {33264137; 33462743}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T16:44:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 827,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 770,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124930,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 402,
        "headingId": 770,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>They present as a solitary unpigmented nodule {33264137; 33462743}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T16:44:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 828,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 771,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124931,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 402,
        "headingId": 771,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The age distribution is wide, ranging from children to the elderly {33264137}. Tumours with an ACT::<em>MITF</em> translocation show a female predilection.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T16:44:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 829,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 772,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124932,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 402,
        "headingId": 772,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T16:44:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 830,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 773,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124933,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 402,
        "headingId": 773,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>MITF</em> fusions are the oncogenic driver. MITF is a transcription factor and member of the MiT/TFE family, which also contains TFEB, TFE3, and TFEC and acts as the master regulator of melanocytic differentiation {16899407}. The actin gene involved in the ACT::<em>MITF</em> translocation can be <em>ACTB</em> or <em>ACTG1</em> {33264137}. The large C-terminal MITF fragment of the fusion protein retains its transcriptional activation domain, suggesting hyperactive MITF function {33264137}. <em>MITF</em>::<em>CREM</em> fusions are an alternative driver and may drive expression of the non-rearranged <em>MITF</em> allele, resulting in hyperactive MITF function {33462743}. CREM is a member of the CREB1/ATF1/CREM family of transcription factors that regulate diverse cellular processes, including transcription of MITF. The N-terminal MITF portion of the fusion protein lacks its transcriptional activation domain, whereas the transcriptional activation domain is preserved in the CREM fragment, analogous to the <em>EWSR1</em>::<em>ATF1</em> and <em>EWSR1</em>::<em>CREB1</em> fusions found in clear cell sarcoma.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T16:44:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 837,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 780,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124934,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 402,
        "headingId": 780,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>See <em>Clinical features</em>, above.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T16:44:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 831,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 774,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124935,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 402,
        "headingId": 774,
        "headingText": "<p>The tumours are primarily dermal with common subcutaneous extension, and they lack an intraepidermal component. There is a distinctive splaying of the reticular dermal collagen bundles, creating a checkerboard-like pattern. The tumour cells are large, with copious optically clear or occasionally granular cytoplasm. The cytomorphological features are variable; morphology ranges from low-grade to higher-grade with nuclear enlargement, nuclear contour irregularity, and nucleolar enlargement. Necrosis, ulceration, perineural involvement, and lymphovascular involvement are not typical, and mitotic activity is inconspicuous {33264137; 34958499}.</p>\r\n<h5>Immunohistochemistry</h5>\r\n<p>The tumours are positive for HMB45 or melan-A (MART1) and usually for S100. MITF is strongly positive {33264137}.</p>\r\n<h5>Differential diagnosis</h5>\r\n<p>Perivascular epithelioid cell tumour (PEComa), melanoma (either metastatic or primary dermal), and clear cell sarcoma represent the main differential diagnoses. S100 expression is typically not prominent in PEComa {18277881}. Clear cell sarcoma should have <em>EWSR1</em>::<em>ATF1</em> or <em>EWSR1</em>::<em>CREB1</em> fusions {8401579}, whereas cutaneous melanoma typically has MAPK pathway driver mutations.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T16:45:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 832,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 775,
    "contributions": [
